it used to be that when you were an  author you hoped your book would hit the  new york times bestseller list if it did  your book sales would skyrocket  traditional outlets like the new york  times once wielded power and prestige  they could influence everything from the  books you buy to the people you vote for  some say but the power of mainstream  media is waning  although cnn saw a considerable spike in  viewership during the capital riots as  it tends to do when there's breaking  news their audience numbers on most days  are abysmal  when cnn touted in this press release  howard beat out msnbc in views in april  2022 it ironically revealed that its own  numbers weren't that great cnn's  flagship shows often bring in less than  200 000 daily views in the key 25 to 54  demographic  yet even their views online are often  less than those of independent youtubers  why are powerful networks with millions  of dollars of backing behind them  getting fewer views than some individual  content creators  i thought about my time working as a  television reporter most recently in  london a new day in a new prime minister  good morning anne-marie i've compared  that with my work now as an online  content creator there's a massive  difference between videos that are made  by tv networks and videos that are made  by youtubers even though both are video  oriented online content creators have a  lot more creative freedom which often  makes for more compelling content i can  make a six minute video about the  difficulty of landing starship or a 23  minute video about the dark web crime  kingpin of silk road  you don't get the luxury of that much  time with television take the example of  interviews you might notice that the  format of web-only interviews is  different from interviews made for tv  that are then released online when joe  rogan speaks to guests for his podcast  the conversation seems natural and  unscripted and often lasts anywhere from  two to four hours  whereas tv interviews are time sensitive  because they have to move on to another  segment it has to be solidarity i have  to end it there because we're out of  time we'll be back  as a result interviews often sound like  sound bites or may appear inauthentic  even when tv feels authentic you're  still restricted by time  in this hilarious segment from global  news the canadian network where i used  to work one of the hosts made an  artichoke dip and it went horribly wrong  but there's no vinegar in there so  here's what happens what  that's like all that i can do  oh my god  okay so  like it's caught i'm not trying to be  rude here it's okay i agree with you  this was fantastically entertaining and  could have gone on for longer but they  had to cut to a commercial break or  another segment i felt these time  constraints myself my stories were only  one minute and 30 seconds long because  so many stories have to be squeezed into  the nightly newscast usually an hour  long  time constraints hurt storytelling  because you can't delve deeply into a  topic  shows such as 60 minutes offer longer  form reporting but they don't air daily  because they take considerable resources  to produce when daily tv reports are  later uploaded online their short rigid  format fails to compel people to watch  for example my report about a zero-waste  grocery store in the uk may have gotten  hundreds of thousands of views when it  aired on television stations in north  america yet it only received 600 views  on youtube when uploaded by a local  chicago station or take bloomberg  technology's youtube channel they simply  re-upload segments from their tv program  online the result is just a few thousand  views per video  it's hard for networks to produce  content that works well for online  because their storytelling is beholden  to the format for television and the  advertising dollars it brings you'd be  hard-pressed to find a network willing  to do an in-depth review of the iphone  sc like on mkbht's tech channel or a  12-minute video about why black balls  are floating on a reservoir like on  veritasium science channel because tv  audiences don't have long attention  spans for that kind of storytelling  so why is it that when cnn focused on  online video by launching a streaming  service it flopped  cnn plus was touted as the netflix of  news cnn's hired hundreds of people and  created dozens of new shows for the  service it featured original series  documentaries news shows and live daily  programming instead of the 2 million  subscribers that the head honchos at cnn  anticipated after a year  cnn plus reportedly drew fewer than 10  000 views a day  the service shut down in april after  just one month  pretty much no one wanted to pay 5.99 a  month to watch news shows where they can  watch videos on youtube for free  even netflix is struggling to retain  subscribers partly because of price  hikes and again cnn plus shows still  maintained the familiar format of a tv  broadcast which doesn't work well for  the web  although the brains behind cnn plus felt  it stood out because of its interactive  feature where viewers could ask  questions  it really wasn't anything unique  reddit has been doing that for nearly  two decades  cnn banked on its brand name to carry  the day but it was a humiliating failure  traditional outlets used to be the  gatekeepers of information if you wanted  to know what was going on in the world  you got your news from the radio your  local newspaper or on tv  now anybody can be a reporter  some argue this opens the door to  disinformation as professional  journalists are the ones trained to fact  check however there are also accusations  that media outlets can't be trusted  either this media bias chart created by  the company ad fontes media shows the  perceived political leaning of various  news outlets  instead of despairing about their demise  media organizations have to reinvent  their format or face certain death when  every